**Amendment (Declared computational hiding)** · `amend:identity:hiding-property` Every admission record declares the fields it hides or explicitly declares none. Every recipe with a nonce or salt input must make that declaration regardless of whether its consumer claims privacy. This adds a conditional property to (`tab:identity:properties`) and every class in (`tab:identity:class-properties`): computational hiding of the declared fields, conditioned on the public projection, lengths and expressly disclosed metadata. An admission names adversary knowledge, the primitive and entropy assumptions, entropy floor, freshness, custody, disclosure transition and non-claims. Collision resistance alone does not establish hiding. Evidence pins the construction, independently reviews the assumption, witnesses entropy-failure and reuse refusals when generation is implemented, checks secret custody and removal, and refuses unintended disclosure. Fixed vectors and statistical output tests prove neither entropy nor cryptographic hardness. (`gate:identity:implementation`) checks the declaration, assumptions, scoped witnesses and held obligations, not a computational-hardness theorem. The existing marker-policy, storage-payload, recognition-disclosure, graph archive-notice and public observation-artifact identities declare hidden fields: none; they commit public inputs or bytes and use no concealment nonce or salt. A later concealing identity cannot inherit that declaration merely by reusing their primitive.


