**Decision (Authentication key binding)** · `dec:identity:authentication-keys` The author-token binding in (`amend:identity:author-token-binding`) supersedes the direct verification-key target of this decision. An Actor is the permanent run-qualified asset identity, and signing keys are replaceable delegates selected by its public token history and prospective network retirements. No accepted identifier, burn or history is reassigned. Key loss or disclosure is a tolerated delegate fault when the token remains controllable; loss of the token control key has no recovery promise.

**Decision (Host authority binding)** · `dec:identity:authentication-host` A host identity is pn-host-bip340-v1/ followed by the lowercase hexadecimal x-only host key. The selected run's public deployment configuration names exactly one expected host identity; callers must supply that selection independently of a candidate handshake. The disclosed host key must equal that identity, and the run in the authored identifier must equal the selected run. A seal cannot nominate its own trusted host. The public record retains that run configuration with the authentication evidence so an offline reader can repeat the comparison; choosing which run configuration to trust remains the deployment premise, not a consequence of a signature by its own key. This chooses a fixed host per run over an online certificate hierarchy or mutable host registry. Host-key rotation requires a later explicit deployment transition; this edition supplies neither rotation nor authentication of finalized anchors, and a valid seal promises no inclusion. Host and Actor keys have distinct roles even if their public bytes happen to coincide; the three signed messages remain role-separated.

**Definition (Verified header name)** · `def:identity:verified-header-name` Let B be exactly the canonical array projection of pn-act-profile/act-proposal/v1 under (`amend:identity:act-proposal-admission`), including its existing bounds, family vocabulary and currently empty protocol-reference list. V is pn-interchange's canonical unsigned-key CBOR document: key 0 is the text org.peer-network.test.verified-act, key 1 its version, key 2 B, key 3 [proposal_recipe,P], key 4 actor_proposal_signature, key 5 [content_recipe,C_Y], key 6 [dependency_recipe,C_D], key 7 content_edition and key 8 dependency_edition. All keys are required. The repository maintainers reserve this test namespace at major 1, minor 0 for this header, separately from the complete act-envelope namespace. For this authentication edition both producer and verifier require exactly [1,0,0]; any other version is an Edition refusal. General interchange acceptance excludes patch from semantic identity, but this authentication operation restricts the representation to one version spelling so one header value has one signed encoding. Structural interchange acceptance of another version does not admit its authentication operation. Recipe identifiers and editions are CBOR text; residues are byte strings of exactly 32 bytes; the signature is a byte string of exactly 64 bytes. The three recipe strings are exactly pn-act-profile/act-proposal/v1, pn-act-profile/act-content/v1 and pn-act-profile/act-dependency/v1. This authentication edition admits the content and dependency edition texts 1 and 1 only. B preserves authored integer pairs, canonical structural list order and both legs, never a reduced rational or a host-local handle. The complete header theory and independent vectors must be published before the first external producer is enabled under (`schema:interchange:global`) and (`sig:interchange:theory-assignment`); the enclosing complete act document remains separately held under (`open:identity:act-envelope-allocation`). No unknown content key is in the initial closed theory. This reservation records the design, not a registry implementation or completed allocation evidence. This uses one encoder and refusing decoder under (`req:xchg:determinism`) and (`conv:xchg:refuse-never-repair`), rather than JSON, a second CBOR codec or signing a repaired representation. No epoch, position, time, salt, nonce, payload, dependency opening or approval is inside V. This fixes deployment bytes for network-v0.31.1, ``def:graph:verified-act``; it is not a paper-mandated serialization. realizes{``amend:identity:act-proposal-admission``; The header reuses the exact typed projection and its bounds, not its concealment assertion.}

**Definition (Authentication material)** · `def:identity:authentication-material` Under pn-act-authentication/author-token-bip340/v1, public_verification_material is the canonical internal CBOR array [authentication_edition,run,actor_key,host_identity,host_key], with run and both keys exact 32-byte strings and the other fields text. The array shape is retained but its authority interpretation supersedes the direct Actor target/key equality: (`amend:identity:author-token-binding`), (`rule:identity:author-key-history`) and (`rule:identity:author-chain-view`) establish membership of the disclosed Actor key hash. The edition must equal the separate field; unknown editions refuse without aliases. No private key or mutable lookup supplies authority. The array carries the key, not a claim that its mere presence authorizes the act; complete public chain and retirement evidence is required under (`rule:devnet:author-public-evidence`). All signatures retain the exact 64-byte BIP-340 representation. Decode V without repair, compare its envelope and seven content fields against the act and disclosure, including the complete author in B, all three recipe/residue pairs, Actor signature and both editions, and require complete consumption. Retain the exact supplied canonical bytes through removal; distinguish originally requested states from later removal states. The host binding of (`dec:identity:authentication-host`) is unchanged.

**Amendment (Authentication recipe gate)** · `amend:identity:authentication-recipe-gate` The three admissions below are design decisions under (`gate:identity:implementation`), with independent Actor proposal, host realization and Actor approval authority boundaries. They amend the gate's adoption data, not its cryptographic evidence requirement: no signing or verification operation is enabled until (`open:crypto:dependency-admission`) and the first public-verification cut of (`preview:design:authentication-cuts`) are discharged. Their named producer and consumer migration is one implementation series, completed before any production ordering uses this edition; missing implementation refuses, never falls back to presence checks. Authentication keys sign only messages computed inside admitted recipe operations with distinct recipe identifiers and fixed domain tags, never a caller-chosen digest or raw challenge. The proposal operation computes P from its admitted framed proposal recipe and signs P itself; seal, approval and retirement compute their own SHA-256(F(r,...)) messages. A generic sign-bytes entry point, including for capsule access or account workflows, is not admitted for these keys. This is a signer-side conformance requirement, not a property a verifier can infer from a 64-byte signature. F is the injective framing of (`amend:identity:act-framing`); SHA-256 remains the admitted RustCrypto operation. BIP-340 internal challenge hashing belongs to the base-library signature operation under (`dec:crypto:base-library`). Binding rests on the existing commitment assumptions and signature unforgeability, not on direct equality implying authority. All three signed objects are public and declare no additional hidden fields; the only concealment claim is the existing Y/D claim conditioned on (`amend:identity:act-concealment`). Recipe changes assign fresh identifiers, and before 1.0 replacement rebuilds available inputs under (`amend:identity:pre-one`); unavailable s