**Amendment (Case closure admission)** · `amend:identity:case-closure-admission` Under (`req:identity:admission-record`) and (`rule:identity:hash-citation-audit`), the existing calibration case-closure recipe is named same-file-case-closure/v1. Its owner is the CI case-identity implementation in ci/devperf.py. The validated object is a unique named Python or Rust case, its exact body and attributes, its transitive same-file references, its own class fixtures and referenced same-file assignments, constants and statics. The existing producer sorts the reached definition keys and serializes their key-and-source pairs as compact Unicode JSON, encodes UTF-8 and computes SHA-256; this admission changes no recipe bytes or calibration digest. The assurance is source-provenance computational equality under collision and second-preimage resistance, not semantic equivalence or evidence identity. Hidden fields: none. External library code, inherited fixtures from other files, environment and executed results remain outside the declared closure. The review carrier binds operation, path, claim, gloss, label assessments and a closure identity whose recipe, function and digest fields are exhaustive, with only an optional public_commit locator. Unknown fields, recipes, unsafe paths and ambiguous roots refuse before comparison. The validating producer uses the existing case_identity function; witness_binding validates the complete carrier and witness_currency establishes recomputability before the tracker consumes its currency. The present consumers are calibration currency and assertion-review currency: only a current reviewed closure supports a witnessed-label route. The digest is compared only within this typed role and recipe, never as a protocol or report identity. A current review recomputes from its carrying tree, which remains public after import re-signing. A stale review must instead recompute from a named public ancestor and supplies no witness; an unrecomputable review refuses. Any closure change requires re-review or an explicit stale historical binding. Any projection or primitive change requires a new recipe identifier and re-review, never silent reinterpretation. Equality proves neither correctness, entailment, authenticity nor complete dependency coverage.

**Amendment (Author token binding)** · `amend:identity:author-token-binding` This withdraws the direct-key identity and no-delegate-rotation interpretation of (`dec:identity:authentication-keys`) and the target/key equality in (`def:identity:authentication-material`). The authentication edition is pn-act-authentication/author-token-bip340/v1; pn-act-authentication/bip340/v1 remains a distinct historical design, never an alias. The target in pn-devnet-v1/run/target is the Elements asset identifier, in the lowercase hexadecimal display order of the admitted codec, while run remains the existing 32-byte run atom. Binary asset identifiers in this contract are the 32 bytes obtained by decoding that display spelling; consensus serialization byte order is converted explicitly, never guessed. The asset identifier is the creation-fixed value that consensus follows through transfers. An issuance transaction hash identifies a container that may create several assets and changes with transaction details; an issuance outpoint needs the issuance derivation to identify the asset; another digest adds a recipe without adding authority. The asset identifier is therefore selected without an extra repository hash. Its external derivation is admitted by (`amend:identity:author-asset-recipe`). Control of the output holding the token is the root authority over this author; a named signing key is a delegate, not that authority and not the identity. The creation and qualification evidence is (`rule:devnet:author-token`). The same target is the permanent burn destination under (`amend:devnet:burn-targets`) and (`rule:harness:burn-targets`); a token transfer changes delegated authority over the same author, never moves burn between addresses. From this authentication edition, a new author target is a qualified asset identity and nothing else; no new arbitrary or direct-key target can be assigned. Previously accepted arbitrary targets, including direct-key targets, retain their old meaning. Assignment under another binding means historical assignment in the accepted record as of the admitting epoch. A token whose asset identifier collides with such an assignment refuses admission rather than claiming the old identifier or its burn. Fresh qualified targets must be unassigned in that view before funding; a predictable asset identifier cannot be reserved by creating a new non-token target. Creating a token is not Registration, standing, title, human identity or uniqueness of a person. Loss of its controlling key has no recovery mechanism; a later recovery or algorithm transition belongs in this record and must preserve old verdicts.

**Amendment (Author asset locator admission)** · `amend:identity:author-asset-recipe` Admit the external design recipe elements/initial-asset-id/v1 under (`gate:identity:implementation`) and (`req:identity:admission-record`), not its implementation. The object is the issuance prevout (transaction identifier and output index) together with the initial issuance contract hash, identifying the asset consensus follows through transfers. The recipe owner is pn-wallet's bounded chain-layout adapter; the issuing wallet produces the identifier, while token qualification and public authentication replay recompute it and refuse a mismatched claimed asset or author target. The benefit is correspondence with the chain's existing asset locator, rather than an extra repository digest or a transaction identifier that conflates several issuances. Assurance class: artifact binding of the exact issuance inputs under the external SHA-256 construction's binding assumption, not authenticated provenance or qualification by itself. Serialize the prevout as its 32 transaction-identifier bytes in consensus order followed by the unsigned output index in four little-endian bytes, without the transaction input's issuance or pegin flag bits. Compute H = SHA-256(SHA-256(those 36 bytes)), with ordinary finalized hashes. Let M(left,right) initialize SHA-256 at its standard initial state, compress the single 64-byte block left || right, and return the resulting eight state words as 32 big-endian bytes, without padding or finalization. The entropy is M(H,C), where C is exactly the initial issuance contract field's 32 bytes, not a fresh hash of that field or its display spelling. The asset's internal bytes are M(entropy,Z), where Z is 32 zero bytes; this is the two-leaf fast Merkle construction, not finalized SHA-256 or SHA-256d of either concatenation. Reverse those asset bytes for the lowercase hexadecimal display spelling and for the decoded display-order binary identity of (`amend:identity:author-token-binding`). The producer follows the admitted Elements codec; independent conformance vectors and replay recomputation use the reviewed RustCrypto SHA-256 route, including its compression primitive with the state and no-padding rule above, not a second call to the Elements asset helper. The first token-evidence cut implements producer and consuming comparison together, with independent vectors and wrong-prevout, contract, byte-order and finalization controls before operation admission. A changed prevout, contract field, serialization, compression rule or display conversion makes the result inapplicable; an unsupported recipe or missing recomputation evidence refuses the claimed binding. A changed external derivation requires a new recipe and authentication edition with explicit replacement from public evidence, never reinterpretation or reassignment of old identifiers and burn. There are no hidden fields. The result alone proves no unique human, token qualification, control, delegate authority, Registration, standing, title, inclusion or chain finality. This is the fixed external chain-locator use of (`amend:crypto:wallet-codec-features`), not permission for a new repository semantic digest; primitive and operation evidence remain required before implementation.

**Amendment (Signing key hash admission)** · `amend:identity:signing-key-hash` Admit the design recipe pn-act-authentication/signing-key-hash/v1 under (`gate:identity:implementation`) and (`req:identity:admission-record`), not its implementation. Its exact preimage is the ASCII recipe text, one zero byte, then exactly 32 opaque bytes intended as a BIP-340 x-only key encoding; its digest is RustCrypto SHA-256. Parse and validate the point before using it as a verification key. The token holder produces the digest for the on-chain key declaration; the shared authentication verifier and offline replay recompute it from disclosed keys to decide delegate membership. The benefit is compact public delegation without placing a raw key in the chain declaration; direct key storage is an alternative but does not implement the selected hash-only contract. Assurance class: artifact binding of the canonical key encoding under collision resistance, not identity or proof of possession. The hash operation refuses any length other than 32 but does not parse a curve point. Membership and signature verification first parse the disclosed key through the admitted base library and refuse an invalid point before testing membership. A declaration of bytes that are not a key can authorize nothing. A recomputed hash absent from the active set refuses ActorBinding and grants no delegate authority. A malformed declaration has only the effect specified by (`rule:devnet:author-key-output`), not authority inferred from its tag. The hash contains no run or author: shared delegation is intentional, and the act messages bind each author separately. A changed key, encoding, hash or domain is inapplicable; a different recipe requires a new authentication edition and declaration tag. Before external use the producer, decoder and verifier migrate together with independent vectors and wrong-key/domain/length controls under (`amend:crypto:authentication-entry`); missing operation admission refuses. Old declarations are never reinterpreted. No secrecy, token control, chain finality, Registration or inclusion follows from a digest. There are no hidden fields in this recipe.

**Rule (Author key history)** · `rule:identity:author-key-history` Begin at the qualified issuance, which is the first transaction of the token history and may name keys. Follow only the unique explicit unit of that asset through its spends, ordered by chain block height and transaction position, to the selected prefix. For each transaction on this path collect every valid declaration under (`rule:devnet:author-key-output`); output position orders the evidence but membership is a set. The active set is their union from the currently effective retirement transaction inclusive. With no retirement it starts at issuance. A transfer with no key output preserves the set; repeat declarations add no duplicate authority. When several qualified tokens move in one transaction, every valid declaration applies to each token whose input meets the all-outputs signature commitment of (`rule:devnet:author-token`). A spend outside that admitted script requirement makes only that token's path unsupported: its later authority views are unavailable, even if a subsequent output is explicit, while old keys and accepted verdicts are not deleted and other tokens remain independently trackable. Confidential outputs unrelated to the token unit or tagged declarations do not break its path under the consensus-validity premise. Later paths separate: neither another token's earlier history nor its later transactions are inherited. This is not transitive union of author sets. A key occurring both before and at or after the retirement boundary remains active through its later declaration. The holder may move the token to a new address to add delegates; that move alone retires none. Retiring all delegates requires a later history transaction naming no keys, then a retirement selecting it, with no later declarations in the selected prefix. The resulting empty set authorizes no act until a subsequent token move names a key. Its identity and prior history remain intact.

**Rule (Authentication chain view)** · `rule:identity:author-chain-view` Eligibility for an act admitted in epoch k uses the one settled chain prefix bound to that epoch's recognized marker snapshot, under (`amend:storage:marker-settlement`) and (`rule:design:marker-recognition`), plus retirements effective before that epoch. The deployment marker interpretation must identify the exact block hash and height of its chain prefix; if it cannot, token authentication is unavailable, not a read of the current tip. Token transfers need not be accepted burn events: scan the chain prefix, not the burn accepted set. Check the declared stability buffer and finality premises for that prefix; missing or contradictory evidence prevents readiness. Proposals and seals may be checked provisionally against a named earlier view, but ordering rechecks the admitted epoch view. At one fixed prefix and retirement state the verdict is deterministic. Extending a valid token history can add authority, never remove it: no-key transfers, duplicate keys and unrelated transactions cannot turn a valid delegate into an invalid one. New key declarations can change an earlier absent-key refusal into membership for a later admission view. Retirement can remove authority only prospectively; a later explicit re-declaration can restore it. An unsupported confidential or destroyed token path makes later views unavailable, not a deletion of old keys or old verdicts. A reorganization of an unpinned candidate changes only provisional results; replacement of the settled prefix violates the inherited finality premise and halts the run without rewriting accepted acts. Availability is a deployment premise, not a consequence of inclusion or a signature.

**Amendment (Network key retirement)** · `amend:identity:key-retirement` Retirement is an authenticated deployment record beside the act graph, not a new authored-act family. The normative families at network-v0.31.1, premise{``rem:nodes:edge-census-normative``; the existing family census excludes a retirement act} and premise{``tbl:nodes:act-payload-schema``; payload meanings exclude a retirement side effect}, assign Registration to profile data and the control families to their own graph relations; interpreting their payload as key retirement would add an authority side effect absent from that meaning. Deployment key management is permitted by premise{``rem:graph:authentication-realization-out-of-scope``; authentication realization belongs to the deployment}. A retirement record names run, complete author string, the selected marker identity, the epoch of acceptance, and a transaction identifier proposed as a cutoff. Its membership in that token's own prefix history determines effect, not record acceptance, under (`rule:identity:retirement-order`). It is signed by a key active in that epoch's pre-retirement view. This chooses an already delegated signature over a generic proof of control of an arbitrary Elements output script, which would need a new script-challenge protocol. If every delegate is lost, the holder first moves the token and names a new key, waits for that transfer's settled admission view, and uses the new key to retire the old prefix. Disclosure may let a delegate submit a competing retirement, but cannot change token ownership or the permanent identity; the holder can always add a later delegate while its token remains controllable. No instant recovery or acceptance-time guarantee is claimed.

**Rule (Retirement order and signature)** · `rule:identity:retirement-order` The host retains accepted retirement records in the epoch's complete public deployment-record list, committed with the same immutable epoch result and marker reference. The list is canonical by author, then transaction identifier bytes, then record bytes; identical records deduplicate. Record acceptance checks bounded canonical representation, edition, run, epoch and marker binding, active-key authorization and signature against the epoch's established starting authority view; malformed or unauthorized records refuse. It does not look up the proposed cutoff transaction or wait for it to appear. The host uses the already established authority view to check the signer; this does not waive that view's availability prerequisite. Every accepted record remains in the public list, including a no-effect record. Its effect is judged once against the accepting epoch's fixed chain prefix and starting retirement state, never against another record in the list: a transaction absent from that author's own path, including a transaction beyond that prefix, or a position before the effective cutoff, has no effect. A no-effect record can never activate in a later view; a new record must be submitted for that view. This avoids deferred instructions changing authority without a new public acceptance event. A boundary may stay equal or advance on that token's path, never retreat. At the next epoch boundary the greatest effective position for each author becomes effective; the named transaction's own declarations stay active. Acts ordered in the accepting epoch and all earlier epochs retain their verdicts. This avoids a circular choice of same-epoch act eligibility, retirement order and standing, and creates no Layer-1 edge, pricing or standing contribution. The complete list and its placement are required public evidence; an omitted list is unavailable, not an empty set. Publication binding remains separately held under (`open:design:dependency-opening-recipe`). Recipe pn-act-authentication/key-retirement/v1 signs SHA-256(F(r,PN-key-retirement-v1,[authentication_edition,run,author,marker_identity,accepting_epoch,transaction_id])), with r that recipe, F the existing (`amend:identity:act-framing`), authentication_edition and author CBOR text, run a 32-byte CBOR byte string, marker_identity the publication theory’s records-burn-Marker value embedded directly as a CBOR value rather than a byte string containing its encoding, accepting_epoch a CBOR unsigned integer in the canonical framing of (`amend:identity:act-framing`) rather than publication decimal text, and transaction_id a 32-byte CBOR byte string in display order. The verifier takes the marker value unchanged from the published package; the storage projection never enters this signed recipe. A later publication theory version that changes this value’s encoding requires a new retirement recipe edition: signed bytes never change meaning. This is a design admission under (`req:identity:admission-record`): the delegate produces it; host acceptance and offline reconstruction consume it to compute the boundary effect; its benefit is a publicly attributable prospective instruction and its assurance class is provenance. Failed representation or authorization refuses the record; a valid signature admits the record, not its claimed cutoff membership. Absent or backward cutoff references produce the deterministic no-effect outcome above, not a refusal or a future retry. Offline reconstruction requires the complete fixed-view evidence to distinguish no effect from unavailable evidence. Any changed field, key membership, edition or frame invalidates applicability. Migration assigns fresh recipe/edition and preserves old records; independent message, boundary, shared-key and malformed evidence vectors precede implementation under (`gate:identity:implementation`). It contains no private fields and claims no chain spend, inclusion, finality or instantaneous revocation. The public list representation and its placement are designed in (`amend:design:epoch-authentication-fields`); that publication representation carries the unchanged recipe inputs with the disclosed signer and signature, and does not change this recipe or its effect rule.

**Amendment (Shared-key signed objects)** · `amend:identity:shared-key-messages` This amends (`amend:identity:authentication-recipe-gate`), (`amend:identity:proposal-signature`), (`amend:identity:host-seal`) and (`amend:identity:approval-signature`) for the token edition: replace their direct-key authorization prerequisite with the selected public token-history membership, without changing their message bytes or recipe identifiers. In particular the proposal retains Sign(P) as specified in (`amend:identity:proposal-signature`), with the explicit signer-side key-use restriction of (`amend:identity:authentication-recipe-gate`); public verification alone does not establish that restriction. P already commits B including the complete ActId.author, so two authors sharing a key have different proposal messages under the admitted binding assumptions. V includes B and P; the host seal signs those exact V bytes, and approval signs V and that seal. No author is inferred solely from a key or its hash, and no signature can be transplanted between authors without failing the message binding. The retirement recipe likewise includes the complete author. The authentication edition changes because authority interpretation changed, not because the three existing signature frames changed. The host remains the explicitly configured run key of (`dec:identity:authentication-host`): making host authority transferable by an author token would add a separate deployment-governance transition without solving this Actor fault. Old edition records are not silently upgraded; new ordering supports only the explicitly selected token edition after its admission evidence exists. realizes{``amend:identity:authentication-recipe-gate``; The token edition changes authorization while preserving message bytes and the signer restriction, not claiming private entropy.}

